WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.100
- We met Sian Jason, a
student and former prisoner,

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.810
who formed an incredible connection

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.590
while meeting once a week
to discuss literature.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.240
Jason, we clearly saw how this
benefits the students, but

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.630
what does it do for the people in prison?

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.370
- Well, hi, everybody.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.093
You know, in 1999,

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:19.450
I committed a terrible crime

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.000
and I was sentenced to 26
years to life in prison

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.660
as a result.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.590
And at the time the like
the general attitude

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.570
about people with life sentences was that

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.960
the only way that they'd
get out was in a pine box.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.330
And, you know, there was
a lot of hopelessness,

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.210
a lot of, you know, poor treatment

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.850
that resulted from that way of thinking,

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.510
a lot of inhumanity.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.810
I had a lot of support from
my family and loving mother.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.840
And I made the decision early on,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.820
that I wasn't gonna continue
to bring pain to her life.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.770
So I started making better decisions,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.650
started pursuing my education
and finding other men

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.250
who wanted to do something
positive with their time.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.930
And by the time that Palmer came in,

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.070
it was really like the
first time in almost,

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.630
I'd already been incarcerated
for almost a decade.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:02.930
And Palmer started coming in

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.230
and the students gave us the first

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.030
like real encounter with
people who viewed us as people.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.210
You know, their willingness to come in

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.980
and read books with us,

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.900
to exchange thoughts and ideas

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.080
kinda prompted us to be open

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.750
and share our vulnerabilities with them.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.070
And along the way,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.197
you know, we were reminded that,

00:01:24.197 --> 00:01:25.890
"Hey, you know, our lives mattered"

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.160
and that there were
people in the community

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.640
who actually cared

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.810
- Such a beautiful
thing this program, wow.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.170
- Yeah, and by the way,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.210
Jason, I've become quite
good friends with Jason.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.260
I mean, he's probably
the most educated person,

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.210
or one of the most educated people I know.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.170
He has two master's degrees,

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.970
- Wow!
- That he got in prison.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.580
You know, just he's one example of

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.030
the incredible humanity
that exists behind bars.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:54.940
I mean, this isn't,

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.930
you know, there are certainly people

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.140
who have to suffer the consequences

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.010
of the crimes that they committed.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:01.670
But there are people who

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.880
again, they are incredibly remorseful

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.930
and they have committed their lives

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.820
to trying to help other people.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.210
And Jason, now that he's been out

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.910
I mean, he truly has dedicated his life

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.890
to trying to help people
who are incarcerated

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.700
and people who have gotten out of prison

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.670
and to try and help reform
the criminal justice system.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:22.830
- Man, you have any thoughts on this?

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.663
- I mean, I'm just,

00:02:23.663 --> 00:02:27.160
I'm humbled and in awe
this program is amazing,

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.300
And Jason

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.670
you know, I respect that you

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.440
took responsibility for your actions,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.000
but, you can hear it in your voice,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.420
you can see it in your demeanor,

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.370
how you evolved.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.500
A lot of people don't know this

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.900
but my father ran with the
Black Panthers in the 60s.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.280
And he got himself into some trouble.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.490
And he ended up doing
four years in San Quentin.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.430
And that's where he had time to think,

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.850
make decisions, reform himself.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.490
And he came out a different person.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.650
Not perfect, but seeking basic goodness,

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.240
seeking a better life for himself,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.240
but then bigger than himself.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.050
And you seem very similar, you know?

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.920
So congratulations to your second chance,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.760
second chance and I love,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.880
I love that you're helping the youngsters.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.880
I love that it's bigger than yourself.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.480
The fact that you're giving back

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.753
is huge man and so much respect to you.

